[00:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'My name is Noel Michaels. For the last month, I\'ve been creating video games with artificial intelligence. If you\'re interested in using artificial intelligence to create video games, here\'s what you need to know. This video will cover certain meta-lessons that you will have to learn one way or another . First of all, don\'t'

[00:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'use Unreal Engine, okay? Just don\'t do it. Choose Unity or Godot. I\'ve heard good things about Godot. It seems to be an open source engine. I used Unity to create what you see here: a color-'

[00:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'folding puzzle game, my attempt at a tactical shooter, and then a horse racing game. Let me clarify again. Don\'t use Unreal Engine. I love Unreal Engine games, but this is just a big piece of crap, okay? He is so unpleasant to work with.'

[00:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Your agent will be disappointed working with him. A very simple task will take forever to complete. You will spend tokens and money. Just don\'t use Unreal Engine, okay? I try to keep it high-level,'

[00:50] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'but if you want to learn more about these lessons and maybe my thoughts on some of them, I\'ve written a few articles in my free school community, The New Digital Creativity. Join us if you are a creative person who is not afraid to use new'

[01:02] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'technologies to implement your ideas. I should clarify that all of this advice is, in my opinion, true as of mid- September 2026. Things will change, and things will change quickly, but yeah, don\'t use Unreal Engine for now. I don\'t think Unreal'

[01:14] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'will get an update for about a year and a half . So during this time I can\'t imagine this being a good place to work with AI game development . The second thing you need to know is that AI development is all about iteration. For example, you'

[01:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'are going to explain your idea to GPT or Claude . You\'ll get goosebumps because he\'ll understand your idea perfectly, and it\'ll give you so much confidence. He will say, "Sure, I can build it ." This will work for 20'

[01:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'minutes, 30 minutes, 45 minutes, maybe an hour. Then you open the game and it will be so horribly awful. This will not be good at all. Well, there\'s no such thing as creating a good game in one go,'

[01:50] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'at least not now. It doesn\'t matter how detailed your tip is. The first thing a bot creates will be terrible. Video game development is all about iteration, right? Artificial intelligence development is all about iteration. And that can'

[02:03] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'be quite unpleasant. The best way I can describe it is this: if you ask him to build you a house, he will say, "Oh, sure, I can build you a house." Then you will load the game and see a cube with a pyramid on top.'

[02:15] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Technically, did a bot make you a house? Well, yeah, okay, I guess so. But is that what you meant? Obviously not. And this will happen at every step. It doesn\'t matter how well you communicate'

[02:27] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'with the bot, the important thing is to iterate on what it creates. Iterations at every step. I simply cannot emphasize this enough . This will take you a lot of time, right? But it\'s also a kind of magic. Want'

[02:39] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'to see what can happen in 2 days in AI game development ? When I was creating my racing game, look at the bot\'s first attempt. I actually asked Claude to do this demonstration, and look how ridiculous it all looks. It\'s just'

[02:51] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'terrible. And you know what? The AI ​​was so proud of itself. He created a game about racing. Technically, yes, that was the case. But is this what I wanted? Is this something anyone would ever want? No. If you spend'

[03:03] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'a couple of days iterating , you can get something like this. Is n\'t that so interesting? I think I should make a separate video about how I created all these drawings and pixel sprites, but I\'ll try to keep the lessons in this video at a fairly high level.'

[03:15] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Please let me know if you want me to dive deeper into how I actually did these specific things. Anyway, don\'t use Unreal Engine and don\'t iterate, okay? The third thing you need to know is how to iterate properly. If you'

[03:27] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'want to succeed with artificial intelligence, you need to constantly take screenshots. This is how you talk to a bot. This is how you communicate more effectively, because your words won\'t get you far. You literally need to take'

[03:39] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'a picture of your computer, it\'s called a screenshot, you need to circle what you want to change, and you need to send that picture along with your words to the bot to explain what\'s going on. It could be the distance between two resources, it'

[03:51] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'could be an overlap, it could be a misrendering of the resource, it could be a resource offset where you literally draw an arrow and then draw a circle around where you want it to be . Communicating using screenshots . That\'s how I designed'

[04:04] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'everything I\'ve ever created with AI. And it\'s extremely easy to do on a Windows computer. You press Ctrl, the Windows key, and the letter S at the same time. This will open the screenshot tool. Then you click and'

[04:16] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'drag what you want to do, and then you can draw on top of that right after . On Mac, things are a little different. I honestly hate the built-in screenshot tool . I don\'t think you should use it. I do n\'t use it. I use this free software'

[04:28] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'called Shutter. You can download it. This is an application. It installs on your computer and is a shortcut. I think it\'s Command Shift plus the number two. I mean you can customize it, I\'m sure. But clicking this shortcut opens an easy-to- use'

[04:40] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'screenshot tool , just like in Windows. And that\'s how you communicate with the bot, with your words and a visual map of your instructions. This is literally how I designed everything I\'ve ever created with AI. Fourth, you need to know that you will need an'

[04:52] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'expensive subscription. I don\'t think you\'ll get anything with $20 a month. Your 5-hour limit will be exhausted very quickly. Your weekly limit won\'t last long. I switch between $ 100 and $200 plans quite often'

[05:04] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '. If you are deeply immersed in development, you will need the most expensive plan, such as 20X. It costs about 200 US dollars per month. But if you\'re just starting out on a project, I think the $100/month plan will be fine for you. And I'

[05:16] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'just want to say that this is the best offer in the history of mankind. I don\'t care if it seems expensive to you. Just think how much work you could get done for $100 a month. Well, man, if you could pay a person $100 or'

[05:28] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '$200 a month and they would work on your video game literally 24/7, never complaining, always willing to help, what are we even talking about? This is such a good deal. This is the best deal of'

[05:40] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'all time. The amount of intelligence that can be bought for 200 bucks is simply incredible. You can access a smart person every second. It\'s worth it. These are business expenses. Another thing you need to'

[05:52] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'know about developing video games with artificial intelligence is to have some idea of ​​the level of effort involved. For example, when you have GPT or Claude loaded at the bottom, you will see something called effort level. You can literally tell the bot how'

[06:04] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'hard it should work on your request. But do you know how wild a choice this is for your clients? Imagine you go to a restaurant and the waiter asks you what your level of effort would be. Is this a tricky question? Why do I have'

[06:16] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the option to ask for more effort? In any case, let me explain this as clearly as possible. I think you should think about it this way. How much of your subscription do you want to spend on this task? And this is not at all a literal'

[06:29] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'technical definition of effort level. But for those just starting out, if you\'re curious, I think that\'s all you need to know. Because if you just ask what the weather will be like today , you don\'t need to go to extreme lengths. Anything you consider an'

[06:41] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'easy task, reduce the level of effort. And then, if you\'re not happy with the results, that\'s when you start to increase your efforts. Does this make sense? Perhaps the best advice is this. Start from the middle. Maybe it\'s'

[06:53] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'medium, maybe it\'s high, because there\'s super high and ultra high. So whatever is average on your screen. And what I like to do when I\'m not getting the results I want is I flip it all the way to the right . I\'m moving to ultra.'

[07:05] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'And I literally ask the bot to conduct an independent audit. I tell him what happened, where things went wrong, and that I increased my level of effort, and ask what he would do differently now . I don\'t know if this is all a little too complicated for this'

[07:17] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'video, but the thing about ultra-effort and these higher levels of effort is that when you use them, the bot spawns sub-agents. And that\'s what makes everything last that much longer. Have you ever heard this term before? Subagents? This is where things'

[07:29] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'can get a little sci-fi . All this means is that you see one chatbot that you are talking to, but it starts talking to more chatbots that you don\'t see. This is an agent who has agents and he hands out a bunch of different tasks.'

[07:42] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'And these kinds of things only really happen at these higher levels of effort. It does the absolute maximum amount of work for you, which means it uses more and more of your token limit. I was working on my tactical game and trying to come up with'

[07:54] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'placeholder names for items and locations. Nothing definitive, I just wanted the game to have a slightly more completed visual aesthetic so I could get a sense of what it looked like. I told the bot that I was hiring him as creative director and that I'

[08:06] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'needed a dossier for this world I was creating. The bot on Ultra created a bunch of worker bots to write various bibles for this world. One relied on sports analogies, one was a little more abstract, one was super sci-'

[08:18] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'fi, and then the bot I was talking to actually looked at all the work done by the subagents, picked the best one, and this is what it presented to me. Is n\'t that funny? Probably a bit exaggerated. So again , you'

[08:30] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'have to be careful when using these higher levels of effort. I do n\'t know what number we\'re in , but another thing you should know is that it\'s actually a good strategy to start making these AIs check each other. For example, I have a subscription to GPT'

[08:42] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and Claude, you would be surprised at the progress that is made when you take one of GPT\'s answers and give it to Claude, or vice versa. And you literally say, "What could be better about this?" For some reason, progress seems to be'

[08:54] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'going well. And that\'s exactly how I like to do it. When I have problems and, you know, something doesn\'t work, I tell the bot: "Hey, I\'m going to go ask my friend for help. What does he need to know? And this will force the bot to write a'

[09:06] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'summary of everything it has been working on. He will lay out the obstacles. And then I go to another service, you know, GPT or Claude, and I say, “Hey, my friend is working on a video game. Here\'s what\'s happening. How would you help him? Then this'

[09:18] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'bot will write a bunch of things . And then you return that response to the service you started with. You say, " Hey, I asked a friend and he said to try this." And then you just paste the message that another bot sent you. It\'s super amazing how'

[09:30] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'well this works. I\'m not going to lie. I do n\'t know why this is happening. Theoretically, you could just start a new chat with the same model and maybe it would work . Maybe it\'s all just placebo. But again, for some reason, if you'

[09:42] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'show AI work to another AI, it seems to improve. I\'m definitely not the first to notice this. I\'ve seen this on the Internet before. All I can say is that I think it works. So, if you don\'t mind increasing your monthly'

[09:54] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'AI budget, one of the first things I would try is to consider using two subscriptions from different providers. And on that note, I just want to clarify that my theory is that it\'s a context issue where the bot'

[10:06] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'can\'t see the forest for the trees. I don\'t know if you\'ve ever heard this phrase before, but it refers to how a forest clearly looks like a forest from the outside. But when you\'re in the forest, all you see are the trees. You don\'t really have the'

[10:18] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'full picture of the situation. So in a similar situation, you might be working with a bot that has been living in your project for a few days, and it might be blinded by all the context surrounding it. And honestly, context is a completely different story.'

[10:30] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'I don\'t have any advice on how to manage this well. Except for maybe one thing, make sure your agents document everything. That\'s what an MD file is for. Have a design document. Have an'

[10:42] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'ideas document. Have a document of the problems. Have an economics document. If you write any notes and send them to the bot , make sure those notes are recorded somewhere, okay? The agent should take care of everything. And then future'

[10:54] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'agents will be able to view and read all the notes that previous agents worked on. And speaking of notes, you should download Obsidian. OK? This is the best free software I have ever used. This is my favorite note-taking software,'

[11:06] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'hands down. It is local. It\'s on your computer. I take notes in Obsidian. I copy the relevant parts that I want to discuss with the bot. I literally say to the bot, "Hey, I have some notes for you." I put a colon and then insert'

[11:18] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the notes I want the bot to review. And then I say, " Please document this, and then come and talk to me." This is the exact process I used to create everything I\'ve ever created with artificial intelligence. Go into Obsidian, create a folder for your project,'

[11:30] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'make a note about your project, use headings to separate different parts of the note, track all your ideas and thoughts offline, and then paste whatever you want into GPT or Claude as you work. Another important idea about'

[11:42] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'video game design, especially when it comes to artificial intelligence, is that you need to find fun as quickly as possible, okay? And it starts with trust in the process. For example, you will get angry at artificial intelligence. You will be disappointed. Sometimes it'

[11:54] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'will seem to you that everything is meaningless. But I\'m sure you\'ll wake up the next day and have an idea of ​​how to move forward. This has happened to me many times, and as long as you move forward, you\'re fine. But'

[12:06] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'your game has to be interesting, okay? Until you find that fun, that should be your only goal. There\'s no point in creating a world, creating art , creating an atmosphere, an aesthetic, if there\'s no interesting game to'

[12:18] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'build on. Have you ever seen prototypes of famous video games? They look terrible because the developers know that even if the game looks good, it won\'t matter. It should be interesting. Don\'t continue creating if you can\'t determine what\'s'

[12:30] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'interesting. If you can\'t find something your brain finds interesting in about two or three days, you need to revisit the entire project. I seriously understand that. Why waste your time? Don\'t waste your money or time on something that isn\'t'

[12:42] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'interesting. Find the fun as quickly as possible, then enjoy the rest of the experience building on that fun. I had a difficult task with my horse racing game. At first she was uninteresting. For example, how can you make a person'

[12:54] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'influence the speed of a horse? And how to make this dynamic? Should a bad horse be able to beat a good horse if the user has perfect data? What does it look like? What makes this interesting? There\'s no point in creating a game that isn\'t interesting. And I'

[13:06] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'really don\'t think you\'re going to suddenly find satisfaction on day 20 or day 90. Finding pleasure should be your main goal, and you need to hunt for it as quickly as possible. This is what this hunt looked like for my horse'

[13:18] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'racing game. The question was, how do we take user data and apply it to results? The user does something, and if they do it well, the horse goes faster, right? Good. Now, how do we make this interesting? I tried'

[13:31] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'horizontal scrolling and input mapping, thinking of DDR or American Idol if any of you beauties played that game around 2003, but it wasn\'t much fun, so I had to keep trying. I also tried this experiment with the countdown. Honestly, that\'s about where'

[13:43] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'we stopped. I made four or five different attempts at the same 5 seconds of gameplay. That\'s what all the work is about. So, how do you find satisfaction? This is the next thing you need to know. You need to get this AI to build a lab for you'

[13:55] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '. You need to have a safe space in your game for experimentation. Let me give you an example. Let\'s talk about Pokemon for a second. Let\'s say you\'re creating a game about Pokemon. You turn on the game, approach a'

[14:07] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'random trainer, start a battle, and realize that your Pokémon is simply destroying its own. This is not very fun. Instead of closing the game, editing some values, maybe giving your opponent a new Pokemon, you need a lab'

[14:19] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'inside the game so you can make all these changes much faster. Let the AI create a lab section for you where you can go in and edit any values ​​in the game. You may be able to customize'

[14:31] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'your Pokémon\'s moves. You may be able to adjust the strength of these movements. You may be able to change the opponent\'s Pokémon, levels, and values ​​of all the moves involved. You need to be able to do this inside the game so you'

[14:43] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'can iterate quickly. This is how I tested my horse racing systems. For this clock spinner, I have all the parameters on the side. How fast it spins, its momentum, acceleration,'

[14:55] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the distance between the slices within which you need to stop the needle. Everything you need to find pleasure should be set up by you, and it should be as simple as possible. Create a setting and'

[15:07] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'lab system that you can experiment with inside your game. OK? For example, instead of trying to find enjoyment in horse racing, and I say, "Okay, make the timeline 20% slower," waiting for the bot'

[15:19] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'to rebuild it, and then evaluating the result, it creates a laboratory. This is a specialized test where I can open the settings panel, I drag sliders while the game is running, the changes are applied immediately, I launch it again and feel'

[15:31] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the difference. I can save presets, I can compare them, I can randomize them, I can reset everything. You shouldn\'t just create a game, you should create tools to discover what the game wants to be. And I told you this video was'

[15:43] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'going to be a little more meta, that\'s what I mean . These aren\'t the clearest instructions, because they will mean something different to everyone watching. I don\'t know what your game looks like, I don\'t know what your lab will look like , but this is the process necessary'

[15:55] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'to succeed when you\'re creating with AI. You won\'t be able to describe the pleasure in a clue. It just does n\'t work that way. But you have to be able to find pleasure in using sliders. Build in parameters to find'

[16:07] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'satisfaction. Another thing you need to know is that AI cannot have vision. Do you understand what I mean? He may understand you perfectly well , but he doesn\'t really extrapolate very well. He is not building to achieve a greater goal. He has no'

[16:19] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'vision of where he should go. That\'s what you exist for. You are a producer. You know what you are trying to create. You know when you look at something whether it\'s what you wanted or not. AI doesn\'t know this. He doesn\'t'

[16:31] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'have this skill. Perhaps this is what distinguishes AI from humans. We can see things that don\'t exist yet. AI understands this in theory and in text, but has no practical way to manifest it'

[16:43] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'in reality. And you need to remember this, otherwise you will be disappointed. OK? You are a producer. You have to give the AI ​​what it needs, and what it needs is your vision, both'

[16:55] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'literally and spiritually. It is quite obvious that it can follow your thoughts, but it does not have its own personal thoughts. It will never tell you, "Actually, I thought so." It will never have'

[17:07] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the desire to fight for an idea. It will never defend itself. You can so easily overturn AI decisions, at least for now. For example, if you were wondering what color the wall should be in your game and you said,'

[17:19] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'let\'s start with white. It would answer, "Oh yes, white." Of course, it will look great. But what if you said, " Oh no, I actually thought the walls were supposed to be covered in leopard skin?" It would say, "Oh, you\'re right." It fits'

[17:31] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the atmosphere of the game so perfectly. Really? Really. If you turned around and said, “Oh, leopard skin looks terrible. Let\'s go back to white." It will immediately turn over. It will never defend one of its decisions.'

[17:43] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'And you need to understand this, otherwise you will be disappointed, because this discrepancy is quite easy to miss. Okay, let\'s move on to some quick lessons. You need to develop the habit of making backups. Fortunately, this is'

[17:55] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'very easy to do. There is a website called GitHub that literally lets you store backups for free. Forever. As much as you want, it\'s completely confidential. This is the best that can happen. And your agent should be able to'

[18:07] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'handle all of this for you if you just ask them to set up GitHub. For example, setting up a backup system so you can freely experiment and roll back if necessary. This is my recommendation. Free software, the'

[18:19] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'best software. Nice to use. Choose GitHub. Next, one thing I like to do, since I know the agent has collected all my notes at this point, I like to ask, what does he want to do next? And you will be surprised. He usually has a'

[18:31] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'pretty good idea of ​​what prioritization should look like. Especially for a project like a video game. Don\'t be afraid to ask the AI, for example: "Hey, what do you want to work on next?". This is a good trick. Only one agent should be running in your game at a time'

[18:43] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '. I mean, it\'s good to separate chats by function, okay? You can create a project inside GPT and then have different chats for different features in your game. For example,'

[18:55] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'one chat for creating drawing styles. You can have one chat to discuss the economy in your game . One chat for dreaming about future ideas. And that\'s fine, but you really shouldn\'t have multiple agents working on the'

[19:07] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'same file at the same time. For example, if you have an agent fixing a drawing on one of your levels, don\'t let another agent mess with the economy at the same time. They can intersect and get confused. This is'

[19:20] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'definitely one of those things you\'ll have to learn on your own, so trust me. Try to only allow one agent to work on the game at a time. Another tip you might want to know is to ask the agent to create a'

[19:32] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'transfer file, as when the chat gets too long you start to see the contacts getting compressed, and this can be a bit annoying as it means the AI might forget some things. So you need a transfer file that you can present to the'

[19:44] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'next agent. Essentially, he will be explaining what his role in the production has been up to this point and likely what is to come next. You are handing over the work. That\'s when you start a new chat and pass that file to the'

[19:56] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'new agent. New contacts. And honestly, I think that\'s pretty much all you\'d learn if you started making video games using artificial intelligence. If you are interested in this, please join us in the new digital creativity, my'

[20:08] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'free community on the school platform. You will be able to communicate and learn from like-minded, creative enthusiasts who are not afraid to use new technologies to implement their ideas. I hope I see you there. Good luck. Have fun.'

[20:20] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'I hope you are well. Take care of yourself. And see you next time. Peace.'
